  "billing_notes": [
    " context caching billing for repeated request prefixes, with a default TTL (time-to-live) of 5m. For TTL settings and cost optimization tips, see ",
    " support batch inference for large-scale tasks that do not require real-time responses, billed at 60% of the standard price.",
    " to get the exact token count of each call.",
    "1 MTok = 1,000,000 tokens. All prices above are per MTok consumed.",
    ": uploading and extracting a document alone incurs no file API charges. For tips on improving cache hit rates and choosing a TTL tier, see ",
    "All K2 series models support a 262,144-token context window.",
    "All K3 series models support a 1,048,576-token context window.",
    "Best Practices for Context Caching",
    "Cached input is billed at this rate; each hit automatically refreshes the cache entry's TTL (time-to-live).",
    "Dear Kimi Users, starting October 31, 2026, K2 series models will no longer be available to newly registered users; existing users are not affected. We plan to retire all K2 series models by the end of December 2026, please ",
    "Explanation: Prices exclude applicable taxes. Specific tax obligations are subject to local tax regulations and will be calculated at checkout based on your jurisdiction.",
    "Model inference APIs bill input and output tokens by usage. For K3 series models, cache writes are billed separately by TTL (5min / 1h); cached input is billed at the cache-hit price only, with no additional cache write charge. If you upload a document, extract its content, and pass the extracted content as model input, that content is also billed by usage. File-related APIs (file content extraction and file storage) are ",
    "Model inference on the Kimi API platform is billed on a pay-as-you-go basis, with no subscription plan. It differs from products such as the Kimi membership and Kimi Code. ",
    "Token: the basic unit of text processed by the model. A long word like “antidisestablishmentarianism” may be split into several tokens, while a common short word like “word” usually takes just one. For typical English text, 1 token is roughly equivalent to 3-4 characters. Use the ",
    "Writing a prompt prefix into the context cache with a 1-hour TTL.",
    "Writing a prompt prefix into the context cache with a 5-minute TTL (time-to-live); this is the default tier when no TTL is specified."
